---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="I Audited 343 Websites for AI Search Visibility: Here's What I Found | GeoScore"
  schema={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'I Audited 343 Websites for AI Search Visibility: Here\'s What I Found',
    description: 'A reproducible study of 343 real websites: median GEO score 41/100, 44% rate Critical, and 19 of the top 20 publish an llms.txt. What the data actually shows, and what it cannot show.',
    url: 'https://geoscore.help/blog/auditing-1200-websites/',
    datePublished: '2026-07-21',
    dateModified: '2026-10-04',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'GeoScore' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'GeoScore', url: 'https://geoscore.help' }
  }}
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <article class="prose prose-invert max-w-none">
      <div class="text-center mb-10">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full text-xs font-medium bg-orange-500/10 text-orange-400 border border-orange-500/20 mb-4">
          Case Study
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold mb-3">I Audited 343 Websites for AI Search Visibility</h1>
        <!-- The corpus is 343 sites audited in the first pass; 324 survived the
             re-run after the robots.txt fix, and every score below is from that
             second pass. Both numbers are stated in the methodology section. -->
        <p class="text-gray-400">Published July 21, 2026 · Updated October 4, 2026 · 9 min read · Reproducible study</p>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 text-lg mb-4">
          I built <a href="https://geoscore.help" class="text-geo-400 hover:underline">GeoScore</a>, a free tool that scores a website's AI-search readiness across 11 dimensions. The obvious next question is: <strong class="text-white">what does the score distribution of the web actually look like?</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          This post is the answer, from a run you can reproduce: <strong class="text-white">343 real sites</strong>, audited with the same scoring code the browser audit uses.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-yellow-500/10 border border-yellow-500/20 p-4 mt-4">
          <p class="text-yellow-300 text-sm mb-0">
            <strong>Correction (2026-10-04).</strong> An earlier version of this post claimed 1,200 audited sites and reported a 3.4&times; citation lift. Both numbers were unverifiable — the database held 3 audit rows for a single domain, and a static audit cannot observe whether an AI engine cited anything. The study below replaces them. The original 1,200-site claim is withdrawn.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <div class="card p-6 mb-6 bg-orange-500/5 border-orange-500/20">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold mb-3 text-white">TL;DR</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 list-disc list-inside">
          <li><strong class="text-white">Median score: 41 out of 100.</strong> The typical site is not optimized for AI search, but it is not broken either.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-white">44.4% rate Critical</strong> (under 40). 8.6% reach Good; nobody reaches Excellent.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-white">19 of the 20 highest-scoring sites publish an <code class="text-geo-400">llms.txt</code>.</strong> Sites with one average 60.3; sites without average 38.0.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-white">30.6% publish no <code class="text-geo-400">robots.txt</code> at all</strong>, and only 3.6% of the rest block any AI crawler.</li>
          <li>My own scorer was <strong>wrong about this</strong> and overstated blocking by 25&times; — Finding 4 has the before-and-after.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- METHODOLOGY -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">How I ran the analysis</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          I wanted a sample that could be re-derived rather than a list I happened to have. So the rule is fixed and scripted:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 list-disc list-inside mb-4">
          <li><strong class="text-white">Source:</strong> the GitHub Search API, repositories, sorted by stars descending.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-white">Stratified:</strong> 12 fixed star bands, from <code class="text-geo-400">stars:&ge;50000</code> down to <code class="text-geo-400">stars:50..79</code>, taking the top 100 repositories in each — 1,200 candidates.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-white">Filtered:</strong> only repositories with a homepage, since a site is required. Hosting placeholders like <code class="text-geo-400">*.github.io</code> are excluded.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-white">Deduplicated</strong> by registrable host, so one company counts once.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          That left <strong class="text-white">457 unique sites</strong>. Each was audited with GeoScore's own scoring code, at concurrency 5 with a 15-second timeout; unreachable sites were retried once at 30 seconds.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-white/5 border border-white/10 p-4">
          <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm mb-2"><strong class="text-white">Final sample: 324 scored, 133 unreachable (29.1%).</strong></p>
          <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mb-0">The failures are real and recorded, not discarded — mostly timeouts plus sites that refuse programmatic requests. Every number below comes from the 324 scored sites. That is comfortably above the 100-site floor I set for publishing anything at all.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-4 mb-0">
          A note on what this sample is: these are repositories with visible homepages, so it skews toward developer-facing products. It is not a representative sample of the web, and I am not going to pretend otherwise.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-4 mb-0">
          <strong class="text-gray-300">Two passes, one corpus.</strong> The first run scored 343 sites. After I fixed the robots.txt rule described in Finding 4, I re-ran the same 457 candidates with the corrected scorer; 324 came back. <strong class="text-gray-300">Every score in this post is from that second run</strong>, because it reflects what the tool actually does today. The first run's numbers survive only in Finding 4, as the before half of the comparison. The 19-site difference between runs is network conditions, not a change in method — 126 of the second run's failures were 15-second timeouts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FINDING 1 -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">Finding 1: the median site scores 41</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          The distribution is bottom-heavy, though less so than the first run suggested:
        </p>
        <div class="card p-5 mb-4 bg-white/5">
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 list-none">
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Critical (0–39)</span><span class="text-white">144 sites — 44.4%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Basic (40–69)</span><span class="text-white">152 sites — 46.9%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Good (70–85)</span><span class="text-white">28 sites — 8.6%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Excellent (86+)</span><span class="text-white">0 sites — 0%</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          Median 41, mean 43.4, and the highest score in the entire sample was <strong class="text-white">80</strong>. None of these 324 sites reached the top band, and the lowest was 8.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300">
          The practical read: AI-search optimization is not a mature practice. Whatever the eventual benchmarks turn out to be, almost nobody has done this yet — which is good news if you have not started.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FINDING 2 -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">Finding 2: llms.txt is the clearest dividing line in the data</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          <strong class="text-white">78 sites (24.1%) publish an <code class="text-geo-400">llms.txt</code>.</strong> The gap between them and everyone else is large:
        </p>
        <div class="card p-5 mb-4 bg-white/5">
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 list-none">
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>With llms.txt (n=78)</span><span class="text-white">mean 60.3 · median 64</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Without (n=246)</span><span class="text-white">mean 38.0 · median 38</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          Two ways to see the same split. Of the 67 sites scoring 60 or higher, <strong class="text-white">51 have an llms.txt</strong>. Among Critical sites, only <strong class="text-white">6.9%</strong> have one. The highest score reached by any site without an llms.txt was 72, and <strong class="text-white">19 of the top 20</strong> have one.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-blue-500/10 border border-blue-500/20 p-4 mt-4">
          <p class="text-blue-300 text-sm mb-0">
            <strong>What this is not.</strong> This is a correlation in a convenience sample, not a causal effect, and I am not claiming a "lift." Publishing an llms.txt is a signal that someone has done the thinking about what the site is and which pages matter — and that thinking shows up in other dimensions too. The honest statement is: <em>llms.txt presence marks the sites that have been deliberately optimized, and those sites score about 22 points higher.</em>
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mt-4 mb-0">
          If you want the practice rather than the statistic, the <a href="https://geoscore.help/blog/llms-txt-ultimate-guide" class="text-geo-400 hover:underline">ultimate llms.txt guide</a> covers the spec and the common mistakes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FINDING 3 -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">Finding 3: robots.txt is a very small problem</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          I expected this to be the headline problem. The data says it is close to a non-problem:
        </p>
        <div class="card p-5 mb-4 bg-white/5">
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 list-none">
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Scored sites</span><span class="text-white">324</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Publishes a robots.txt</span><span class="text-white">225 — 69.4%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>No robots.txt at all</span><span class="text-white">99 — 30.6%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Blocks at least one AI crawler <span class="text-gray-500">(of the 225 that publish one)</span></span><span class="text-white">8 — 3.6%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Specifically blocks GPTBot <span class="text-gray-500">(of the 225)</span></span><span class="text-white">5 — 2.2%</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          Eight sites out of 225. Every one of them does it deliberately, with an explicit named group:
        </p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded-lg overflow-x-auto text-sm text-gray-200"><code>User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: ClaudeBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: Googlebot
Allow: /</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mt-4 mb-0">
          The complete list: <code class="text-geo-400">aka.ms</code>, <code class="text-geo-400">hackage.haskell.org</code> and <code class="text-geo-400">weibo.com</code> block all 20 crawlers; <code class="text-geo-400">catppuccin.com</code> blocks 5; and <code class="text-geo-400">hellogithub.com</code>, <code class="text-geo-400">rustdesk.com</code>, <code class="text-geo-400">unity.com</code>, <code class="text-geo-400">maxon.net</code> block one each. Note the denominator: the 99 sites with no robots.txt are <em>not</em> counted as blocked. With no file, a crawler falls back to the permissive default, and folding them in would have produced a headline number roughly 25&times; too large.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mt-4 mb-0">
          One of these is worth a look, because it shows why "longest rule wins" is not the whole story. <code class="text-geo-400">aka.ms</code> publishes <code class="text-geo-400">Disallow: /</code> alongside <code class="text-geo-400">Allow: /fwlink/p/</code>. The Allow is the longer pattern, so a naive longest-match check reads it as "allowed" — but it does not cover the site root, so every crawler is in fact blocked. The question is not which rule is longest; it is which rule governs <code class="text-geo-400">/</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FINDING 4 -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">Finding 4: my own scorer was wrong about robots.txt, and I fixed it</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          The version of this post published earlier reported that GeoScore's <code class="text-geo-400">AI Crawlability</code> dimension scored 323 of 343 sites (94.2%) at 0/12, and I set that column aside instead of quoting it. I have since fixed the dimension, and the fix changes the number so much that the old figure is worth explaining rather than just deleting.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          The old check looked for an explicit <code class="text-geo-400">User-agent: &lt;bot&gt;</code> line containing <code class="text-geo-400">Allow: /</code>. That inverts RFC 9309: a file which <strong class="text-white">never mentions</strong> an AI bot leaves it on the permissive default, and a site only lists a bot when it intends to restrict it. A perfectly ordinary file — <code class="text-geo-400">User-agent: *</code> with <code class="text-geo-400">Disallow: /search?q=*</code> — was scored as a total block. <code class="text-geo-400">dev.to</code>, fully open to GPTBot, scored 0/12.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          The check now parses the file into groups, merges every group naming the bot, ignores the <code class="text-geo-400">*</code> group whenever a specific one exists, and asks a single concrete question: can this crawler fetch the site root? Re-fetching all 337 robots.txt files under both rules:
        </p>
        <div class="card p-5 mb-4 bg-white/5">
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-300 list-none">
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Old rule: sites with at least one crawler blocked</span><span class="text-white">228 — 67.7%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>New rule: sites with at least one crawler blocked</span><span class="text-white">7 — 2.1%</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>Old rule: median AI Crawlability score</span><span class="text-white">0 / 12</span></li>
            <li class="flex justify-between"><span>New rule: median AI Crawlability score</span><span class="text-white">12 / 12</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-0">
          I verified the six sites whose verdict changed by reading their actual robots.txt, and the new answer is right in every case — <code class="text-geo-400">catppuccin.com</code> really does ban GPTBot, ChatGPT-User, Google-Extended, CCBot and PerplexityBot by name; <code class="text-geo-400">rustdesk.com</code> bans CCBot; <code class="text-geo-400">unity.com</code> bans Bytespider. The dimension is no longer a meaningful signal on typical sites, which is the correct outcome: a site that says nothing about AI crawlers is not penalised for it. If you scored your site before this fix, your AI Crawlability number went up, and the change is a correction, not a regression.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- WHAT THE DATA CANNOT SHOW -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">What this study cannot tell you</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          The previous version of this post made four claims I have removed rather than restate:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 list-disc list-inside mb-4">
          <li>"3.4&times; citation lift for sites with a valid llms.txt"</li>
          <li>"FAQ schema 6.1&times; more likely to be cited"</li>
          <li>"HowTo schema 3.8&times; more likely to be cited"</li>
          <li>"the top 3 fixes deliver ~80% of the score improvement"</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          Every one of those is a claim about whether an AI engine <em>cited</em> a site. A static crawler audit cannot observe citation — it fetches robots.txt and HTML, and citation happens inside a model. Measuring it would need a multi-engine citation crawl or an LLM simulation, and the simulation in my own product is an estimate rather than a measurement.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-0">
          The 23-point llms.txt gap in Finding 2 is what I can actually evidence. It is a weaker claim than "3.4&times; lift," and it is the one I am willing to stand behind.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TOP 3 FIXES -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">The three things the data supports</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          Ordered by how strongly the sample separates groups, not by how much score each one moves:
        </p>

        <div class="mb-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">1. Publish an llms.txt (about an hour)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">
            The strongest dividing line in the data: 24.1% of sites have one, and they average 22 points higher (mean 60.3 vs 38.0; median 64 vs 38). Write the Markdown summary at <code class="text-geo-400">yourdomain.com/llms.txt</code> following the spec, then reference it from robots.txt. <a href="https://geoscore.help/blog/llms-txt-ultimate-guide" class="text-geo-400 hover:underline">Full guide here →</a>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">2. Check what your robots.txt actually says (five minutes)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">
            3.6% of sites that publish a robots.txt block at least one AI crawler, and 30.6% publish no robots.txt at all — which is not the same thing, since a crawler with no policy is allowed by default. The one trap: do not add <code class="text-geo-400">User-agent: * / Disallow: /</code> while trying to fix it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">3. Fix the cheapest dimensions first (an afternoon)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-300 mb-2">
            Across the sample, Meta &amp; Social sits at 73% of its maximum and Content Quality at 59% — most sites already do these. AI Crawlability (excluding the caveat above), Structured Data at 13%, and E-E-A-T at 25% are where the room is.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- WHAT I'D DO DIFFERENTLY -->
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-white">What I'd build differently</h2>
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-300 list-disc list-inside">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Ship the sampler before the blog post.</strong> The hard part of this study was not the analysis, it was making the sample reproducible. A rule someone else can re-run turns a claim into a result. I should have written <code class="text-geo-400">tools/build-corpus.mjs</code> first.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Separate measurement from marketing in the file layout.</strong> This data lives in <code class="text-geo-400">data/</code> with a provenance note, and the blog post cites it. Nothing in the post is typed by hand.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white">Check the scorer against sites you already know the answer for.</strong> dev.to is not subtle, and the dimension that mis-scored it is the one I would have trusted least if I had not spot-checked two sites by hand.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-geo-500/10 to-purple-500/10 border-geo-500/30">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3 text-white">Run your own audit</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          The 11-dimension browser audit is free and sign-in is optional. Drop in your URL, get a 0-100 score, a radar chart, and auto-generated fix files (llms.txt, robots.txt, JSON-LD) you can deploy immediately.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
          With a median of 41 across this sample, most sites have room.
        </p>
        <a href="https://geoscore.help" class="inline-block px-6 py-3 rounded-lg bg-geo-500 hover:bg-geo-600 text-white font-semibold transition">
          Audit your site for free →
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- RELATED -->
      <div class="card p-8 mb-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3 text-white">Read next</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 list-disc list-inside">
          <li><a href="https://geoscore.help/blog/llms-txt-ultimate-guide" class="text-geo-400 hover:underline">The Ultimate llms.txt Guide</a> — spec, examples, and 10 mistakes</li>
          <li><a href="https://geoscore.help/blog/what-is-geo" class="text-geo-400 hover:underline">What is GEO?</a> — the complete primer</li>
          <li><a href="https://geoscore.help/blog/geo-vs-seo" class="text-geo-400 hover:underline">GEO vs SEO</a> — how they differ and how they work together</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

    </article>
  </section>
</Layout>
